{"name":"Slumber Sloth","hex":"#cdc5b5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"slumber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very light state of sleep, almost awake.\n\n A very heavy state of sleep.","A very heavy state of sleep."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be in a very light state of sleep, almost awake.","To be inactive or negligent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/slumber"},{"term":"sloth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Laziness; slowness of mind; disinclination to action or labour; a feeling combining indifference and lethargy; a dragging idleness.","Any animal in the suborder Folivora.\n Any member of species of the families Choloepodidae and Bradypodidae of herbivorous, arboreal South American mammals, noted for its slowness and inactivity.\n Any of the extinct group of ground sloths."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be idle; to idle (away time)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sloth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sloth","blocks":[{"text":"Sloths are a Neotropical group of xenarthran mammals constituting the suborder Folivora, including the extant arboreal tree sloths and extinct terrestrial ground sloths. Noted for their slowness of movement, tree sloths spend most of their lives hanging upside down in the trees of the tropical rainforests of South America and Central America. Sloths are considered to be most closely related to anteaters, together making up the xenarthran order Pilosa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Bicho-pregui%C3%A7a_3.jpg/330px-Bicho-pregui%C3%A7a_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sloth","wikidata":"Q2274076"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Choloepus_hoffmanni_%28Puerto_Viejo%2C_CR%29_crop.jpg/500px-Choloepus_hoffmanni_%28Puerto_Viejo%2C_CR%29_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign